  "query_preview": "..\nThe relatively dry trade-wind regime continues, keeping shower \nchances topped at 20 percent throughout the entire forecast period, \nwith the exception of 30 percent on Friday for Guam. Transient\ntrade-wind troughs will continue to move through the islands,\npossibly bringing a slight uptick in showers here and there. Looking\nahead, there is the possibility for a stronger trough to pass south\nof the region, with its outermost showers settling over Guam, mainly\nconcentrated over southern portions of the island. Since this is\nstill six days ahead, much can change from here on out.\n\nAccording to the U.S. Drought Monitor, Guam is abnormally dry (D0)\nwhile the CNMI is unchanged. A strong to very-strong El Nino remains\nthe dominant climate oscillation, meaning a possible earlier onset of\ndrought, as well as the continued potential for tropical cyclones. \nWater conservation is highly encouraged sooner rather than later, \nespecially as dry season approaches. Maintain general storm \npreparedness and weather awareness.",
